• “The list of items is on the table.” (subject = list, not items)
• “Each of the students has a handout.” (each = singular)
• “Everybody wants to pass.” (everybody, anyone, nobody = singular)
• “Mathematics is my best subject.” (looks plural, takes a singular verb)
• “The committee has met.” (treat a group noun as one unit, especially in formal writing)

When you edit, find the subject first, cover the “of …” phrase with your finger, and read the sentence again.', 5, now() - interval '140 hours'),
  (md5('nexa-sample:p-cms-4')::uuid, 'cms-107', null, md5('nexa-sample-user:issah.mohammed')::uuid, 'Respectfully, the CMS lecture is too crowded. More than 400 of us in one hall, and if you sit at the back you can’t hear anything without the microphone. Can the department split the class?', 3, now() - interval '300 hours'),
  (md5('nexa-sample:p-cms-5')::uuid, 'cms-107', null, md5('nexa-sample-user:nii.armah')::uuid, 'Hello. I just finished SHS and I’m waiting for admission. Is it okay if I follow the CMS discussions here to prepare? My English essays were not the best in WASSCE.', null, now() - interval '500 hours'),
  (md5('nexa-sample:p-cms-6')::uuid, 'cms-107', null, md5('nexa-sample-user:kojo.ansah')::uuid, 'Please who has the 2022 CMS 107 end of semester questions?', null, now() - interval '8 hours'),
  (md5('nexa-sample:p-cms108-1')::uuid, 'cms-108', null, md5('nexa-sample-user:gifty.appiah')::uuid, 'Formal letter format for this course (please follow it in the assignment):

1. Your address at the top right (or top left in fully blocked style), followed by the date.
2. The recipient’s title and address on the left, below yours.
3. Salutation: “Dear Sir/Madam,” or “Dear Mr Mensah,”.
4. A heading in capitals or underlined, e.g. APPLICATION FOR THE POST OF LIBRARY ASSISTANT.
5. Body: introduction (why you are writing), two or three body paragraphs, conclusion (what you want done).
6. Complimentary close and your signature, with your full name below it.

In fully blocked format, everything starts at the left margin and there is no indentation. In semi-blocked, the addresses and close are on the right and paragraphs are indented. Choose one style and be consistent.', null, now() - interval '400 hours'),
  (md5('nexa-sample:p-cms108-2')::uuid, 'cms-108', null, md5('nexa-sample-user:adwoa.frimpong')::uuid, 'Summary writing method that got me 9/10 in the tutorial test:

1. Read the passage twice without writing.
2. Read the question again. Are they asking for causes, effects or reasons? Only pick those.
3. Underline one main point per paragraph. Ignore examples, figures and quotations.
4. Write each point as one sentence in YOUR OWN words.
5. Count your words. Don’t go over the limit.

If the question asks for sentences, don’t write in note form.', 4, now() - interval '220 hours'),
  (md5('nexa-sample:p-cms108-3')::uuid, 'cms-108', null, md5('nexa-sample-user:kwame.asante')::uuid, 'Study group for CMS 108: we meet on Saturdays at 10 am at the main library (Sam Jonah Library), ground floor reading area. We practise one essay and one summary each week and mark each other’s scripts. Everyone is welcome.', 4, now() - interval '60 hours'),
  (md5('nexa-sample:p-cms108-4')::uuid, 'cms-108', null, md5('nexa-sample-user:grace.ofosu')::uuid, 'I work in an office and I’m not a student, but I want to improve my report writing. Does this course cover reports and memos? If so, what structure do you use?', null, now() - interval '700 hours'),
  (md5('nexa-sample:p-cms108-5')::uuid, 'cms-108', null, md5('nexa-sample-user:naa.lamptey')::uuid, 'This is not a complaint about the lecturers, but we have had three CMS assignments in four weeks, plus assignments from our other courses. Is it possible to space them out a little?', 3, now() - interval '150 hours'),
  (md5('nexa-sample:p-ilt-1')::uuid, 'ilt-101', null, md5('nexa-sample-user:mawuena.kpeglo')::uuid, 'APA vs Harvard: many of you are mixing the two. Both are author–date styles, and the differences are in the details.

APA 7th edition
In text: (Mensah, 2021) or Mensah (2021)
Reference list: Mensah, K. (2021). Information literacy in Ghanaian universities. Cape Coast University Press.

Harvard (one common version)
In text: (Mensah 2021), with no comma
Reference list: Mensah, K. (2021) Information literacy in Ghanaian universities. Cape Coast: Cape Coast University Press.

In APA, the title is in italics and in sentence case, and the place of publication is not given. For this course, use APA unless the assignment says otherwise.', null, now() - interval '600 hours'),
  (md5('nexa-sample:p-ilt-2')::uuid, 'ilt-101', null, md5('nexa-sample-user:kojo.ansah')::uuid, 'Can someone explain Boolean operators simply? The tutorial question was: find articles on malaria in children, but not in adults.', 4, now() - interval '250 hours'),
  (md5('nexa-sample:p-ilt-3')::uuid, 'ilt-101', null, md5('nexa-sample-user:akua.darko')::uuid, 'The CRAAP test for evaluating sources, in a nutshell:

Currency: when was it published or updated? Is it recent enough for your topic?
Relevance: does it actually answer your question? Who is it written for?
Authority: who wrote it? What are their qualifications? Who published it?
Accuracy: is it supported by evidence? Can you verify it elsewhere?
Purpose: why does it exist: to inform, sell, persuade or entertain? Is it biased?

In the exam they usually give you a website or an article and ask you to evaluate it using all five criteria.', 5, now() - interval '330 hours'),
  (md5('nexa-sample:p-ilt-4')::uuid, 'ilt-101', null, md5('nexa-sample-user:mawuena.kpeglo')::uuid, 'Reminder: the ILT quiz on Friday covers chapters 1–3 only (the information cycle, types of sources, and search strategies). It takes 30 minutes in the ICT centre. Bring your student ID card. You won’t get in without it.', null, now() - interval '30 hours'),
  (md5('nexa-sample:p-ilt-5')::uuid, 'ilt-101', null, md5('nexa-sample-user:kwame.asante')::uuid, 'If you don’t know yet: through the library portal, you can access databases like JSTOR, EBSCOhost, Emerald and ScienceDirect for free on campus Wi-Fi. Ask at the Sam Jonah Library e-resources desk for the off-campus login. It’s much better than searching Google for assignment sources.', 4, now() - interval '900 hours'),
  (md5('nexa-sample:p-ilt-6')::uuid, 'ilt-101', null, md5('nexa-sample-user:afia.boakye')::uuid, 'Honestly, is ILT necessary? A lot of it feels like common sense.', 2, now() - interval '75 hours'),
  (md5('nexa-sample:p-ilt-7')::uuid, 'ilt-101', null, md5('nexa-sample-user:nii.armah')::uuid, 'Can a visitor follow the ILT discussions? I want to learn how to do proper research before I start university.', null, now() - interval '1200 hours'),
  (md5('nexa-sample:p-ilt-8')::uuid, 'ilt-101', null, md5('nexa-sample-user:naa.lamptey')::uuid, 'About the plagiarism checker: changing a few words in a copied sentence still counts as plagiarism. Read the source, close it, write the idea from memory, then compare and cite. That’s real paraphrasing.', 4, now() - interval '160 hours'),
  (md5('nexa-sample:p-mat101-1')::uuid, 'mat-101', null, md5('nexa-sample-user:kwame.asante')::uuid, 'Please help: solve sin 2x = cos x for 0 ≤ x ≤ 2π. I divided both sides by cos x and got sin x = 1/2, so x = π/6 and 5π/6, but the answer sheet has four answers.', null, now() - interval '210 hours'),
  (md5('nexa-sample:p-mat101-2')::uuid, 'mat-101', null, md5('nexa-sample-user:comfort.nyarko')::uuid, 'My office hours for MAT 101 are Tuesdays and Thursdays, 2–4 pm, in the Mathematics department. Come with specific questions and your attempted working. I can help much more when I can see where you got stuck.', null, now() - interval '480 hours'),
  (md5('nexa-sample:p-mat101-3')::uuid, 'mat-101', null, md5('nexa-sample-user:naa.lamptey')::uuid, 'Logarithm laws, with one worked example:

logₐ(xy) = logₐx + logₐy
logₐ(x/y) = logₐx − logₐy
logₐ(xⁿ) = n logₐx
logₐa = 1, logₐ1 = 0
Change of base: logₐx = log x / log a

Example: solve log₂x + log₂(x − 2) = 3
log₂[x(x − 2)] = 3
x(x − 2) = 2³ = 8
x² − 2x − 8 = 0
(x − 4)(x + 2) = 0
x = 4 or x = −2

Reject x = −2, so x = 4.', 5, now() - interval '130 hours'),
  (md5('nexa-sample:p-mat101-4')::uuid, 'mat-101', null, md5('nexa-sample-user:issah.mohammed')::uuid, 'Binomial question: find the term independent of x in the expansion of (2x − 1/x)⁶. I got 160 but my friend says −160.', null, now() - interval '95 hours'),
  (md5('nexa-sample:p-mat101-5')::uuid, 'mat-101', null, md5('nexa-sample-user:adwoa.frimpong')::uuid, 'Sum of a geometric progression: Sₙ = a(rⁿ − 1)/(r − 1) when r > 1, or a(1 − rⁿ)/(1 − r) when r < 1. Both give the same answer.

Example: find the sum of the first 8 terms of 3 + 6 + 12 + …
a = 3, r = 2, n = 8
S₈ = 3(2⁸ − 1)/(2 − 1) = 3 × 255 = 765.', 4, now() - interval '340 hours'),
  (md5('nexa-sample:p-mat101-6')::uuid, 'mat-101', null, md5('nexa-sample-user:kojo.ansah')::uuid, 'Which calculator is allowed in the MAT 101 exam? Can I use my phone calculator?', null, now() - interval '45 hours'),
  (md5('nexa-sample:p-mat101-7')::uuid, 'mat-101', null, md5('nexa-sample-user:nii.armah')::uuid, 'I just finished SHS and want to be ready for MAT 101 before admission. Can I follow along here? What should I revise first?', null, now() - interval '800 hours'),
  (md5('nexa-sample:p-mat101-8')::uuid, 'mat-101', null, md5('nexa-sample-user:akua.darko')::uuid, 'Hot take: past questions alone won’t get you an A in MAT 101. They’re great for seeing the format, but the lecturer changes the questions a little every year. Do the textbook exercises first, then use past questions to practise timing.', 4, now() - interval '560 hours'),
  (md5('nexa-sample:p-mat101-9')::uuid, 'mat-101', null, md5('nexa-sample-user:afia.boakye')::uuid, 'How do you find tan 75° without a calculator?', 3, now() - interval '15 hours'),
  (md5('nexa-sample:p-phy-1')::uuid, 'phy-101', null, md5('nexa-sample-user:kofi.mensah')::uuid, 'A ball is kicked at 20 m/s at 30° above the horizontal. How do I find the range? Take g = 9.8 m/s².', null, now() - interval '180 hours'),
  (md5('nexa-sample:p-phy-2')::uuid, 'phy-101', null, md5('nexa-sample-user:naa.lamptey')::uuid, 'Significant figures summary, since the lab reports keep losing marks for this:

• Non-zero digits always count: 4.56 has 3 s.f.
• Zeros between digits count: 3.05 has 3 s.f.
• Leading zeros never count: 0.00450 has 3 s.f. (the 4, the 5 and the trailing zero)
• Trailing zeros after a decimal point count: 2.50 × 10³ has 3 s.f.

Multiplying or dividing: round to the fewest s.f. 3.2 × 4.56 = 14.592 → 15 (2 s.f.)
Adding or subtracting: round to the fewest decimal places. 12.11 + 0.3 = 12.41 → 12.4', 4, now() - interval '260 hours'),
  (md5('nexa-sample:p-phy-3')::uuid, 'phy-101', null, md5('nexa-sample-user:akua.darko')::uuid, 'Vectors in i, j form, worked example from the tutorial sheet:
A = 3i + 4j, B = −i + 2j

A + B = (3 − 1)i + (4 + 2)j = 2i + 6j
|A| = √(3² + 4²) = 5
|B| = √(1 + 4) = √5 ≈ 2.24
A · B = (3)(−1) + (4)(2) = −3 + 8 = 5
Angle between them: cos θ = A·B / (|A||B|) = 5 / (5√5) = 1/√5, so θ ≈ 63.4°', 5, now() - interval '120 hours'),
  (md5('nexa-sample:p-phy-4')::uuid, 'phy-101', null, md5('nexa-sample-user:kwabena.ofori')::uuid, 'PHY 101 mid-semester exam details:
• Duration: 1 hour 30 minutes
• Covers: measurement and units, vectors, kinematics in 1D and 2D (chapters 1–4)
• Section A has 30 multiple-choice questions. In Section B, answer 2 of 3 theory questions.
• Bring your student ID, a non-programmable scientific calculator, a pencil and an eraser. No phones in the hall.

Venue and time will be on the departmental notice board.', null, now() - interval '70 hours'),
  (md5('nexa-sample:p-phy-5')::uuid, 'phy-101', null, md5('nexa-sample-user:kojo.ansah')::uuid, 'Polite request: the projector in the Science lecture theatre is very dim. From the middle rows, we can barely read the slides during the 8 am lecture. Can it be checked, or can the slides be uploaded before the lecture?', 3, now() - interval '400 hours'),
  (md5('nexa-sample:p-phy-6')::uuid, 'phy-101', null, md5('nexa-sample-user:adwoa.frimpong')::uuid, 'YouTube channels that helped me with PHY 101: Khan Academy (one-dimensional motion and vectors), Professor Dave Explains (short concept videos), and The Organic Chemistry Tutor (lots of worked projectile problems, despite the name). Watch one video, then do the tutorial question on the same topic straight away.', 5, now() - interval '650 hours'),
  (md5('nexa-sample:p-phy-7')::uuid, 'phy-101', null, md5('nexa-sample-user:issah.mohammed')::uuid, 'Is the physics practical a separate course from this one?', null, now() - interval '5 hours'),
  (md5('nexa-sample:p-phy-8')::uuid, 'phy-101', null, md5('nexa-sample-user:kwame.asante')::uuid, 'Debate: should we memorise the equations of motion or learn to derive them? Derivations take time in the exam.', 4, now() - interval '1000 hours'),
  (md5('nexa-sample:p-phy-9')::uuid, 'phy-101', null, md5('nexa-sample-user:afia.boakye')::uuid, 'Quick one: how do I convert 72 km/h to m/s?', null, now() - interval '34 hours'),
  (md5('nexa-sample:p-mat-4')::uuid, 'mat-105', null, md5('nexa-sample-user:kwame.asante')::uuid, 'Anyone want to practise Cramer’s rule and determinants together before the quiz? I’m in hall most evenings after 7.', 4, now() - interval '110 hours'),
  (md5('nexa-sample:p-mat-5')::uuid, 'mat-105', null, md5('nexa-sample-user:naa.lamptey')::uuid, 'How do I use De Moivre’s theorem to find (1 + i)⁸?', null, now() - interval '85 hours'),
  (md5('nexa-sample:p-gen103-2')::uuid, 'gen-103', null, md5('nexa-sample-user:kojo.ansah')::uuid, 'Moments question: a light beam is balanced on a pivot. A 30 N weight hangs 2 m to the left. Where should a 40 N weight go on the right?', 4, now() - interval '140 hours'),
  (md5('nexa-sample:p-gen103-3')::uuid, 'gen-103', null, md5('nexa-sample-user:afia.boakye')::uuid, 'Three problem sets due in the same week as the mid-sem is a lot, please.', 2, now() - interval '22 hours'),
  (md5('nexa-sample:p-gen105-2')::uuid, 'gen-105', null, md5('nexa-sample-user:kwame.asante')::uuid, 'Check my working: 6 Ω and 3 Ω in parallel, then in series with 4 Ω, across a 12 V supply. What is the total current?', 4, now() - interval '190 hours'),
  (md5('nexa-sample:p-gen105-3')::uuid, 'gen-105', null, md5('nexa-sample-user:grace.ofosu')::uuid, 'I do house wiring as a side job and want to understand the theory properly. Can I follow this course here as a visitor?', null, now() - interval '1500 hours'),
  (md5('nexa-sample:p-csc-1')::uuid, 'csc-101', null, md5('nexa-sample-user:kojo.ansah')::uuid, 'Decimal to binary: keep dividing by 2 and read the remainders from the bottom up.
45 ÷ 2 = 22 r1
22 ÷ 2 = 11 r0
11 ÷ 2 = 5 r1
5 ÷ 2 = 2 r1
2 ÷ 2 = 1 r0
1 ÷ 2 = 0 r1
So 45 = 101101₂. Check: 32 + 8 + 4 + 1 = 45.', 4, now() - interval '230 hours'),
  (md5('nexa-sample:p-csc-2')::uuid, 'csc-101', null, md5('nexa-sample-user:naa.lamptey')::uuid, 'The computer lab is always full during practical sessions. Is it okay to bring our own laptops?', 3, now() - interval '55 hours'),
  (md5('nexa-sample:p-eco-2')::uuid, 'eco-101', null, md5('nexa-sample-user:adwoa.frimpong')::uuid, 'Price elasticity worked example. The price rises from GH₵10 to GH₵12, and the quantity demanded falls from 100 to 80.

Simple percentage method: %ΔQ = −20/100 = −20%, %ΔP = 2/10 = 20%. PED = −20% / 20% = −1 (unit elastic).

Midpoint method: %ΔQ = −20/90 ≈ −0.222, %ΔP = 2/11 ≈ 0.182. PED ≈ −1.22 (elastic).

Check which method your lecturer wants, because the two methods can give different conclusions.', 4, now() - interval '270 hours'),
  (md5('nexa-sample:p-eco-3')::uuid, 'eco-101', null, md5('nexa-sample-user:grace.ofosu')::uuid, 'I work in a bank and I’m following this course to understand the economics behind interest rates and inflation. Are there any beginner-friendly book recommendations?', null, now() - interval '1800 hours'),
  (md5('nexa-sample:p-acc-2')::uuid, 'acc-101', null, md5('nexa-sample-user:akua.darko')::uuid, 'The accounting equation keeps everything balanced: Assets = Liabilities + Capital.
Example: you start a business with GH₵5,000 cash (assets 5,000 = capital 5,000). Then you buy equipment worth GH₵2,000 on credit. Assets: 5,000 + 2,000 = 7,000. Liabilities: 2,000. Capital: 5,000. And 7,000 = 2,000 + 5,000.', 5, now() - interval '310 hours'),
  (md5('nexa-sample:p-nur-2')::uuid, 'nur-101', null, md5('nexa-sample-user:adwoa.frimpong')::uuid, 'Anatomy study group: Wednesday evenings at the main library. This week we’re doing the upper limb bones and the muscles that attach to them. Bring your flashcards.', 4, now() - interval '66 hours'),
  (md5('nexa-sample:p-gen101-1')::uuid, 'gen-101', null, md5('nexa-sample-user:kwame.asante')::uuid, 'What to bring to Engineering Drawing: a drawing board, T-square, 45° and 30°/60° set squares, a good compass, 2H and HB pencils (or 0.5 mm clutch pencils), an eraser, masking tape and A3 sheets. Buy them in the first week, because they sell out.', 4, now() - interval '420 hours'),
  (md5('nexa-sample:p-gen101-2')::uuid, 'gen-101', null, md5('nexa-sample-user:issah.mohammed')::uuid, 'What is the difference between first angle and third angle projection? I keep mixing them up.', null, now() - interval '140 hours')
on conflict (id) do nothing;


Your search: malaria AND (children OR child OR paediatric) NOT adults

The brackets group the synonyms together. You can also use quotation marks for exact phrases, e.g. "malaria prevention", and an asterisk for word endings: child* finds child, children and childhood.', null, now() - interval '240 hours'),
  (md5('nexa-sample:p-ilt-2-r2')::uuid, 'ilt-101', md5('nexa-sample:p-ilt-2')::uuid, md5('nexa-sample-user:kojo.ansah')::uuid, 'Ahh, the brackets were what I was missing. Thanks!', null, now() - interval '236 hours'),
  (md5('nexa-sample:p-ilt-3-r1')::uuid, 'ilt-101', md5('nexa-sample:p-ilt-3')::uuid, md5('nexa-sample-user:issah.mohammed')::uuid, 'So Wikipedia fails the test automatically?', null, now() - interval '300 hours'),
  (md5('nexa-sample:p-ilt-3-r2')::uuid, 'ilt-101', md5('nexa-sample:p-ilt-3')::uuid, md5('nexa-sample-user:mawuena.kpeglo')::uuid, 'Not automatically, but its authority is hard to establish because anyone can edit it. Don’t cite it in assignments. Use it to get an overview, then follow its references to the original sources.', null, now() - interval '290 hours'),
  (md5('nexa-sample:p-ilt-3-r3')::uuid, 'ilt-101', md5('nexa-sample:p-ilt-3')::uuid, md5('nexa-sample-user:adwoa.frimpong')::uuid, 'I’d push back slightly: some of the science articles on Wikipedia are more accurate than random blogs that “pass” on currency. CRAAP is a guide, not a scoring machine.', null, now() - interval '280 hours'),
  (md5('nexa-sample:p-ilt-3-r4')::uuid, 'ilt-101', md5('nexa-sample:p-ilt-3')::uuid, md5('nexa-sample-user:issah.mohammed')::uuid, 'That makes sense. Overview from Wikipedia, citations from the real sources.', null, now() - interval '260 hours'),
  (md5('nexa-sample:p-ilt-4-r1')::uuid, 'ilt-101', md5('nexa-sample:p-ilt-4')::uuid, md5('nexa-sample-user:afia.boakye')::uuid, 'Please, Doc, will referencing be in it?', null, now() - interval '28 hours'),
  (md5('nexa-sample:p-ilt-4-r2')::uuid, 'ilt-101', md5('nexa-sample:p-ilt-4')::uuid, md5('nexa-sample-user:mawuena.kpeglo')::uuid, 'No. Referencing is chapter 5, and it will be in the mid-sem.', null, now() - interval '25 hours'),
  (md5('nexa-sample:p-ilt-5-r1')::uuid, 'ilt-101', md5('nexa-sample:p-ilt-5')::uuid, md5('nexa-sample-user:grace.ofosu')::uuid, 'I’m not a student. Is there any free alternative for people outside the university?', null, now() - interval '850 hours'),
  (md5('nexa-sample:p-ilt-5-r2')::uuid, 'ilt-101', md5('nexa-sample:p-ilt-5')::uuid, md5('nexa-sample-user:kwame.asante')::uuid, 'Try Google Scholar, DOAJ (Directory of Open Access Journals) and CORE. They’re all free and have a lot of open-access articles.', null, now() - interval '840 hours'),
  (md5('nexa-sample:p-ilt-6-r1')::uuid, 'ilt-101', md5('nexa-sample:p-ilt-6')::uuid, md5('nexa-sample-user:adwoa.frimpong')::uuid, 'I thought so too until I tried writing a literature review. Knowing how to search a database properly saved me hours.', null, now() - interval '70 hours'),
  (md5('nexa-sample:p-ilt-6-r2')::uuid, 'ilt-101', md5('nexa-sample:p-ilt-6')::uuid, md5('nexa-sample-user:yaw.boateng')::uuid, 'Some parts are common sense, like the library rules. But referencing and plagiarism are not. People lose marks in final year projects because of them.', null, now() - interval '66 hours'),
  (md5('nexa-sample:p-ilt-6-r3')::uuid, 'ilt-101', md5('nexa-sample:p-ilt-6')::uuid, md5('nexa-sample-user:afia.boakye')::uuid, 'Okay, I’ll give the referencing part that. The first two chapters still felt slow to me.', null, now() - interval '60 hours'),
  (md5('nexa-sample:p-ilt-6-r4')::uuid, 'ilt-101', md5('nexa-sample:p-ilt-6')::uuid, md5('nexa-sample-user:ama.owusu')::uuid, 'Fair. The early chapters are slow, but they’re easy marks in the quiz, so I didn’t complain.', null, now() - interval '52 hours'),
  (md5('nexa-sample:p-ilt-8-r1')::uuid, 'ilt-101', md5('nexa-sample:p-ilt-8')::uuid, md5('nexa-sample-user:kojo.ansah')::uuid, 'The “close it” step is the key. Thanks.', null, now() - interval '150 hours'),
  (md5('nexa-sample:p-mat101-1-r1')::uuid, 'mat-101', md5('nexa-sample:p-mat101-1')::uuid, md5('nexa-sample-user:akua.darko')::uuid, 'Don’t divide by cos x, because you lose the solutions where cos x = 0. Factorise instead:

sin 2x = 2 sin x cos x, so
2 sin x cos x − cos x = 0
cos x (2 sin x − 1) = 0

cos x = 0 → x = π/2, 3π/2
sin x = 1/2 → x = π/6, 5π/6

So x = π/6, π/2, 5π/6, 3π/2.', null, now() - interval '205 hours'),
  (md5('nexa-sample:p-mat101-1-r2')::uuid, 'mat-101', md5('nexa-sample:p-mat101-1')::uuid, md5('nexa-sample-user:comfort.nyarko')::uuid, 'Well explained. General rule: never divide by an expression that can be zero. Bring everything to one side and factorise.', null, now() - interval '190 hours'),
  (md5('nexa-sample:p-mat101-1-r3')::uuid, 'mat-101', md5('nexa-sample:p-mat101-1')::uuid, md5('nexa-sample-user:kwame.asante')::uuid, 'Got it now. Thank you both.', null, now() - interval '185 hours'),
  (md5('nexa-sample:p-mat101-3-r1')::uuid, 'mat-101', md5('nexa-sample:p-mat101-3')::uuid, md5('nexa-sample-user:issah.mohammed')::uuid, 'Why do we reject −2? It satisfies the quadratic.', null, now() - interval '120 hours'),
  (md5('nexa-sample:p-mat101-3-r2')::uuid, 'mat-101', md5('nexa-sample:p-mat101-3')::uuid, md5('nexa-sample-user:naa.lamptey')::uuid, 'Substitute it into the original equation: log₂(−2) is undefined, because you can’t take the log of a negative number. Always check your answers in the original equation.', null, now() - interval '118 hours'),
  (md5('nexa-sample:p-mat101-4-r1')::uuid, 'mat-101', md5('nexa-sample:p-mat101-4')::uuid, md5('nexa-sample-user:adwoa.frimpong')::uuid, 'General term: T₍ᵣ₊₁₎ = ⁶Cᵣ (2x)⁶⁻ʳ (−1/x)ʳ = ⁶Cᵣ · 2⁶⁻ʳ · (−1)ʳ · x⁶⁻²ʳ

For the term independent of x: 6 − 2r = 0, so r = 3.
T₄ = ⁶C₃ · 2³ · (−1)³ = 20 × 8 × (−1) = −160.

Your friend is right.', null, now() - interval '90 hours'),
  (md5('nexa-sample:p-mat101-4-r2')::uuid, 'mat-101', md5('nexa-sample:p-mat101-4')::uuid, md5('nexa-sample-user:issah.mohammed')::uuid, 'I see it. I forgot that (−1/x)³ is negative.', null, now() - interval '85 hours'),
  (md5('nexa-sample:p-mat101-4-r3')::uuid, 'mat-101', md5('nexa-sample:p-mat101-4')::uuid, md5('nexa-sample-user:adwoa.frimpong')::uuid, 'Very common mistake. Put the minus sign inside the bracket from the start and it carries through.', null, now() - interval '80 hours'),
  (md5('nexa-sample:p-mat101-5-r1')::uuid, 'mat-101', md5('nexa-sample:p-mat101-5')::uuid, md5('nexa-sample-user:kojo.ansah')::uuid, 'And the sum to infinity? Lecture notes say it only works sometimes.', null, now() - interval '330 hours'),
  (md5('nexa-sample:p-mat101-5-r2')::uuid, 'mat-101', md5('nexa-sample:p-mat101-5')::uuid, md5('nexa-sample-user:adwoa.frimpong')::uuid, 'Only when −1 < r < 1. Then S∞ = a/(1 − r).
Example: 9 + 3 + 1 + …, with a = 9 and r = 1/3: S∞ = 9/(1 − 1/3) = 9/(2/3) = 13.5.', null, now() - interval '320 hours'),
  (md5('nexa-sample:p-mat101-6-r1')::uuid, 'mat-101', md5('nexa-sample:p-mat101-6')::uuid, md5('nexa-sample-user:comfort.nyarko')::uuid, 'No phones. Any non-programmable scientific calculator is fine (for example, a Casio fx-82 or fx-991). Graphing and programmable calculators are not allowed.', null, now() - interval '40 hours'),
  (md5('nexa-sample:p-mat101-6-r2')::uuid, 'mat-101', md5('nexa-sample:p-mat101-6')::uuid, md5('nexa-sample-user:akua.darko')::uuid, 'The bookshop near the main library sells the fx-991. Buy one early and practise with it, so you don’t learn it in the exam hall.', null, now() - interval '36 hours'),
  (md5('nexa-sample:p-mat101-7-r1')::uuid, 'mat-101', md5('nexa-sample:p-mat101-7')::uuid, md5('nexa-sample-user:comfort.nyarko')::uuid, 'You’re welcome. Revise indices, surds, logarithms and basic trigonometry first. Khan Academy’s Algebra 2 and Trigonometry units are free and match the start of the course well.', null, now() - interval '760 hours'),
  (md5('nexa-sample:p-mat101-7-r2')::uuid, 'mat-101', md5('nexa-sample:p-mat101-7')::uuid, md5('nexa-sample-user:kwame.asante')::uuid, 'Also get comfortable with factorising quadratics. It shows up everywhere.', null, now() - interval '740 hours'),
  (md5('nexa-sample:p-mat101-8-r1')::uuid, 'mat-101', md5('nexa-sample:p-mat101-8')::uuid, md5('nexa-sample-user:afia.boakye')::uuid, 'I don’t fully agree. Last year’s paper repeated about 60% of the question types. If time is short, past questions give the most value.', null, now() - interval '550 hours'),
  (md5('nexa-sample:p-mat101-8-r2')::uuid, 'mat-101', md5('nexa-sample:p-mat101-8')::uuid, md5('nexa-sample-user:kwame.asante')::uuid, 'Both are right? The question types repeat, but the numbers and twists change. If you only memorised the solutions, the twist catches you.', null, now() - interval '540 hours'),
  (md5('nexa-sample:p-mat101-8-r3')::uuid, 'mat-101', md5('nexa-sample:p-mat101-8')::uuid, md5('nexa-sample-user:akua.darko')::uuid, 'That’s exactly my point, Kwame. Understand the method, then practise the format.', null, now() - interval '530 hours'),
  (md5('nexa-sample:p-mat101-8-r4')::uuid, 'mat-101', md5('nexa-sample:p-mat101-8')::uuid, md5('nexa-sample-user:comfort.nyarko')::uuid, 'A good discussion. Past questions are useful, but I set questions to test understanding, not memory. Work through the tutorial sheets, then attempt the past papers under timed conditions.', null, now() - interval '500 hours'),
  (md5('nexa-sample:p-mat101-8-r5')::uuid, 'mat-101', md5('nexa-sample:p-mat101-8')::uuid, md5('nexa-sample-user:afia.boakye')::uuid, 'Okay, timed practice it is. Thanks, Prof.', null, now() - interval '490 hours'),
  (md5('nexa-sample:p-mat101-9-r1')::uuid, 'mat-101', md5('nexa-sample:p-mat101-9')::uuid, md5('nexa-sample-user:naa.lamptey')::uuid, 'tan 75° = tan(45° + 30°) = (tan 45° + tan 30°)/(1 − tan 45° tan 30°) = (1 + 1/√3)/(1 − 1/√3) = (√3 + 1)/(√3 − 1). Rationalise: (√3 + 1)²/(3 − 1) = (4 + 2√3)/2 = 2 + √3.', null, now() - interval '12 hours'),
  (md5('nexa-sample:p-phy-1-r1')::uuid, 'phy-101', md5('nexa-sample:p-phy-1')::uuid, md5('nexa-sample-user:kwabena.ofori')::uuid, 'Use R = u² sin 2θ / g.
R = (20)² × sin 60° / 9.8 = 400 × 0.866 / 9.8 ≈ 35.3 m.

While you’re at it:
Time of flight T = 2u sin θ / g = 2 × 20 × 0.5 / 9.8 ≈ 2.04 s
Maximum height H = u² sin²θ / 2g = 400 × 0.25 / 19.6 ≈ 5.1 m

The range formula only applies when the ball lands at the same height it was launched from.', null, now() - interval '170 hours'),
  (md5('nexa-sample:p-phy-1-r2')::uuid, 'phy-101', md5('nexa-sample:p-phy-1')::uuid, md5('nexa-sample-user:kofi.mensah')::uuid, 'Thank you, Doc. So the maximum range is at 45°, because sin 90° = 1?', null, now() - interval '165 hours'),
  (md5('nexa-sample:p-phy-1-r3')::uuid, 'phy-101', md5('nexa-sample:p-phy-1')::uuid, md5('nexa-sample-user:kwabena.ofori')::uuid, 'Yes. At 45°, R = u²/g = 400/9.8 ≈ 40.8 m. Also notice that 30° and 60° give the same range, because sin 60° = sin 120°.', null, now() - interval '160 hours'),
  (md5('nexa-sample:p-phy-2-r1')::uuid, 'phy-101', md5('nexa-sample:p-phy-2')::uuid, md5('nexa-sample-user:kwame.asante')::uuid, 'What about 1200? Is it 2 s.f. or 4?', null, now() - interval '250 hours'),
  (md5('nexa-sample:p-phy-2-r2')::uuid, 'phy-101', md5('nexa-sample:p-phy-2')::uuid, md5('nexa-sample-user:naa.lamptey')::uuid, 'It’s ambiguous. That’s why we use standard form: 1.2 × 10³ (2 s.f.) or 1.200 × 10³ (4 s.f.). Write it in standard form in your answers.', null, now() - interval '245 hours'),
  (md5('nexa-sample:p-phy-3-r1')::uuid, 'phy-101', md5('nexa-sample:p-phy-3')::uuid, md5('nexa-sample-user:issah.mohammed')::uuid, 'Nice. How do I find the direction of A + B?', null, now() - interval '110 hours'),
  (md5('nexa-sample:p-phy-3-r2')::uuid, 'phy-101', md5('nexa-sample:p-phy-3')::uuid, md5('nexa-sample-user:akua.darko')::uuid, 'tan α = j-component / i-component = 6/2 = 3, so α ≈ 71.6° above the positive x-axis. Draw a sketch first, so you know which quadrant it’s in.', null, now() - interval '105 hours'),
  (md5('nexa-sample:p-phy-4-r1')::uuid, 'phy-101', md5('nexa-sample:p-phy-4')::uuid, md5('nexa-sample-user:afia.boakye')::uuid, 'Please, Doc, will the formulas be provided?', null, now() - interval '65 hours'),
  (md5('nexa-sample:p-phy-4-r2')::uuid, 'phy-101', md5('nexa-sample:p-phy-4')::uuid, md5('nexa-sample-user:kwabena.ofori')::uuid, 'No formula sheet. You’re expected to know the equations of motion and the projectile results. Values of constants like g will be given.', null, now() - interval '60 hours'),
  (md5('nexa-sample:p-phy-4-r3')::uuid, 'phy-101', md5('nexa-sample:p-phy-4')::uuid, md5('nexa-sample-user:kojo.ansah')::uuid, 'Thank you. Time to start memorising then.', null, now() - interval '50 hours'),
  (md5('nexa-sample:p-phy-5-r1')::uuid, 'phy-101', md5('nexa-sample:p-phy-5')::uuid, md5('nexa-sample-user:kwabena.ofori')::uuid, 'Thanks for flagging it. I’ve reported the projector to the facilities office. From now on, I’ll upload the slides to Materials the evening before each lecture.', null, now() - interval '380 hours'),
  (md5('nexa-sample:p-phy-5-r2')::uuid, 'phy-101', md5('nexa-sample:p-phy-5')::uuid, md5('nexa-sample-user:adwoa.frimpong')::uuid, 'Thank you, Doc. That will help a lot.', null, now() - interval '370 hours'),
  (md5('nexa-sample:p-phy-6-r1')::uuid, 'phy-101', md5('nexa-sample:p-phy-6')::uuid, md5('nexa-sample-user:grace.ofosu')::uuid, 'I’m a working adult refreshing my physics for a night course. These are very helpful, thank you.', null, now() - interval '600 hours'),
  (md5('nexa-sample:p-phy-8-r1')::uuid, 'phy-101', md5('nexa-sample:p-phy-8')::uuid, md5('nexa-sample-user:akua.darko')::uuid, 'Memorise them, but understand where they come from. v = u + at is just the definition of acceleration rearranged. If you understand that, you won’t mix up the formulas.', null, now() - interval '980 hours'),
  (md5('nexa-sample:p-phy-8-r2')::uuid, 'phy-101', md5('nexa-sample:p-phy-8')::uuid, md5('nexa-sample-user:kofi.mensah')::uuid, 'I just memorise them. In the exam, nobody asks you to derive them, so why spend the time?', null, now() - interval '960 hours'),
  (md5('nexa-sample:p-phy-8-r3')::uuid, 'phy-101', md5('nexa-sample:p-phy-8')::uuid, md5('nexa-sample-user:kwame.asante')::uuid, 'Kofi, the 2023 paper had “Show that s = ut + ½at²” for 4 marks, so derivations do come up.', null, now() - interval '950 hours'),
  (md5('nexa-sample:p-phy-8-r4')::uuid, 'phy-101', md5('nexa-sample:p-phy-8')::uuid, md5('nexa-sample-user:kwabena.ofori')::uuid, 'Both have value. Memorise the four equations so you’re fast. Also be able to derive s = ut + ½at² from the area under a velocity–time graph, because I do ask it. And remember they only hold for constant acceleration.', null, now() - interval '900 hours'),
  (md5('nexa-sample:p-phy-8-r5')::uuid, 'phy-101', md5('nexa-sample:p-phy-8')::uuid, md5('nexa-sample-user:akua.darko')::uuid, 'The v–t graph method is the easiest way. Area of the rectangle (ut) plus the triangle (½at²).', null, now() - interval '880 hours'),
  (md5('nexa-sample:p-phy-9-r1')::uuid, 'phy-101', md5('nexa-sample:p-phy-9')::uuid, md5('nexa-sample-user:kojo.ansah')::uuid, '72 × 1000 m ÷ 3600 s = 20 m/s. Shortcut: divide km/h by 3.6.', null, now() - interval '30 hours'),
  (md5('nexa-sample:p-mat-4-r1')::uuid, 'mat-105', md5('nexa-sample:p-mat-4')::uuid, md5('nexa-sample-user:kojo.ansah')::uuid, 'I’m in. Let’s start with 3 × 3 determinants, because that’s where I make sign errors.', null, now() - interval '100 hours'),
  (md5('nexa-sample:p-mat-5-r1')::uuid, 'mat-105', md5('nexa-sample:p-mat-5')::uuid, md5('nexa-sample-user:yaw.boateng')::uuid, 'Write it in polar form first. |1 + i| = √2 and arg = π/4, so 1 + i = √2(cos π/4 + i sin π/4).
(1 + i)⁸ = (√2)⁸ (cos 2π + i sin 2π) = 16(1 + 0i) = 16.', null, now() - interval '80 hours'),
  (md5('nexa-sample:p-gen103-2-r1')::uuid, 'gen-103', md5('nexa-sample:p-gen103-2')::uuid, md5('nexa-sample-user:nana.addo')::uuid, 'Sum of clockwise moments = sum of anticlockwise moments about the pivot. 30 × 2 = 40 × d, so d = 1.5 m to the right.', null, now() - interval '130 hours'),
  (md5('nexa-sample:p-gen105-2-r1')::uuid, 'gen-105', md5('nexa-sample:p-gen105-2')::uuid, md5('nexa-sample-user:nana.addo')::uuid, 'Parallel: (6 × 3)/(6 + 3) = 2 Ω. Total: 2 + 4 = 6 Ω. I = V/R = 12/6 = 2 A. The voltage across the parallel pair is 2 A × 2 Ω = 4 V, so 0.67 A flows through the 6 Ω and 1.33 A through the 3 Ω.', null, now() - interval '185 hours'),
  (md5('nexa-sample:p-gen105-2-r2')::uuid, 'gen-105', md5('nexa-sample:p-gen105-2')::uuid, md5('nexa-sample-user:issah.mohammed')::uuid, 'The product-over-sum trick only works for two resistors, right?', null, now() - interval '175 hours'),
  (md5('nexa-sample:p-gen105-3-r1')::uuid, 'gen-105', md5('nexa-sample:p-gen105-3')::uuid, md5('nexa-sample-user:abena.sarpong')::uuid, 'Yes, anyone can read along. The Ohm’s law video and the circuit laws notes in Materials are a good start.', null, now() - interval '1450 hours'),
  (md5('nexa-sample:p-csc-1-r1')::uuid, 'csc-101', md5('nexa-sample:p-csc-1')::uuid, md5('nexa-sample-user:afia.boakye')::uuid, 'And hexadecimal?', null, now() - interval '220 hours'),
  (md5('nexa-sample:p-csc-1-r2')::uuid, 'csc-101', md5('nexa-sample:p-csc-1')::uuid, md5('nexa-sample-user:kojo.ansah')::uuid, 'Group the binary digits in fours from the right: 0010 1101 = 2D₁₆. Check: 2 × 16 + 13 = 45.', null, now() - interval '215 hours'),
  (md5('nexa-sample:p-csc-2-r1')::uuid, 'csc-101', md5('nexa-sample:p-csc-2')::uuid, md5('nexa-sample-user:kwame.asante')::uuid, 'Yes, people do. Just install the software from the course page before you come. The lab Wi-Fi is slow.', null, now() - interval '50 hours'),
  (md5('nexa-sample:p-eco-2-r1')::uuid, 'eco-101', md5('nexa-sample:p-eco-2')::uuid, md5('nexa-sample-user:kwesi.arthur')::uuid, 'This is exactly what confused me in the quiz. Our slides use the midpoint method.', null, now() - interval '260 hours'),
  (md5('nexa-sample:p-eco-2-r2')::uuid, 'eco-101', md5('nexa-sample:p-eco-2')::uuid, md5('nexa-sample-user:adwoa.frimpong')::uuid, 'Then use midpoint. Always state the formula in your answer too, because there are method marks for it.', null, now() - interval '255 hours'),
  (md5('nexa-sample:p-acc-2-r1')::uuid, 'acc-101', md5('nexa-sample:p-acc-2')::uuid, md5('nexa-sample-user:issah.mohammed')::uuid, 'And if the owner takes GH₵500 cash for personal use?', null, now() - interval '300 hours'),
  (md5('nexa-sample:p-acc-2-r2')::uuid, 'acc-101', md5('nexa-sample:p-acc-2')::uuid, md5('nexa-sample-user:akua.darko')::uuid, 'That’s drawings. Cash (assets) falls by 500 and capital falls by 500, so 6,500 = 2,000 + 4,500.', null, now() - interval '295 hours'),
  (md5('nexa-sample:p-nur-2-r1')::uuid, 'nur-101', md5('nexa-sample:p-nur-2')::uuid, md5('nexa-sample-user:efua.quansah')::uuid, 'I’ll join. Can we quiz each other on the carpal bones? I always mix them up.', null, now() - interval '60 hours'),
  (md5('nexa-sample:p-gen101-1-r1')::uuid, 'gen-101', md5('nexa-sample:p-gen101-1')::uuid, md5('nexa-sample-user:nana.addo')::uuid, 'Good list. Add a protractor and a scale rule. Use 2H for construction lines and HB for the final outlines.', null, now() - interval '410 hours'),
  (md5('nexa-sample:p-gen101-1-r2')::uuid, 'gen-101', md5('nexa-sample:p-gen101-1')::uuid, md5('nexa-sample-user:kojo.ansah')::uuid, 'Is a mini drafter necessary, or is a T-square enough?', null, now() - interval '400 hours'),
  (md5('nexa-sample:p-gen101-2-r1')::uuid, 'gen-101', md5('nexa-sample:p-gen101-2')::uuid, md5('nexa-sample-user:nana.addo')::uuid, 'In first angle projection, the plan goes BELOW the front view and the left end view goes on the RIGHT. In third angle projection, the plan goes ABOVE the front view and the left end view goes on the LEFT. Always draw the projection symbol in the title block so the reader knows which one you used.', null, now() - interval '130 hours')
